"""What ``Update`` does to every bone — transcribed from the game's IL.

Generated by ``tools/extract_shape_update.py``; do not edit by hand.

``cf_custombody`` / ``cf_customhead`` say which *curve* a slider plays and which
channels it fills.  They do not say which bone the curve reaches, and no bundle
does: the join is ``ShapeBodyInfoFemale.Update`` / ``ShapeHeadInfoFemale.Update``,
27 KB and 5 KB of hand-written IL.  §7.30 recovered the relation from that
stream and left the arithmetic to a bind-pose search; this is the arithmetic.

Each entry is ``(bone, setter, (expr, ...))``, applied in this order, and each
expression is a tuple tree:

===================================  =======================================
``("c", v)``                         literal
``("s", curve, kind, comp)``         ``dictSrc[curve].vct{Pos,Rot,Scl}[comp]``
``("k", key, kind, comp)``           ``correctValue[key]`` (``shapecorrect``)
``("p", bone, comp)``                ``bone.parent.lossyScale[comp]``
``("correctHeadSize",)``             0.91 on male cards, else 1
``("correctNeckSize",)``             ditto
``("headCorrectValue",)``            ``1 / (1 + correctValue[cf_s_head].scl.y)``
``("tb", a, b)``                     ``typeBone ? a : b`` (card flag)
``("+"|"-"|"*"|"/", a, b)``          arithmetic
===================================  =======================================

*kind* is 0/1/2 for position, rotation (degrees) and scale; *comp* is x/y/z.
Setters mirror Illusion's ``Transform*Ex``: ``Scale`` / ``Position`` /
``Rotation`` take three components, ``PositionY`` and friends write one and
leave the rest of the bone's local value alone.
"""
